## What the Substack says\
The post argues that the AI bottleneck has shifted from model intelligence to workflow structure. It says plugins exist to package a skill with tool access, live integrations, deterministic checks, the team\'92s failure modes, and the parts of the standard nobody has written down.[2]\
\
## What plugins should contain\
According to the post, a useful plugin should include:\
- The relevant workflow skill.\
- Access to the tools needed to do the work.\
- Live integrations with the systems the work depends on.\
- Deterministic checks to verify correctness.\
- The team\'92s failure modes, so the agent knows what commonly goes wrong.\
- The unwritten parts of the standard, meaning the practical knowledge people usually keep in their heads.[2]\
\
## Main takeaway\
The core idea is that a plugin is not just a feature wrapper; it is a reusable workflow package that lets the agent operate without needing constant human guidance.[2]\
